Always prefer Server Actions over API Routes for data mutations and server-side operations in Next.js. Server Actions provide better type safety, simpler code, and improved performance.

## Why Server Actions Over API Routes?

### Benefits of Server Actions
- **Type Safety**: Direct TypeScript types without manual serialization
- **Simpler Code**: No need to create separate route handlers
- **Better Performance**: Reduced client-side JavaScript bundle size
- **Automatic Revalidation**: Built-in cache invalidation with `revalidatePath()` and `revalidateTag()`
- **Progressive Enhancement**: Forms work without JavaScript
- **SEO Friendly**: Better for server-side rendering and form submissions

## When to Use Server Actions

✅ **Use Server Actions for:**
- Form submissions and data mutations (CREATE, UPDATE, DELETE)
- Database operations (Prisma queries)
- File uploads and processing
- Email sending
- Data validation and transformation
- Any server-side logic that modifies data

❌ **Use API Routes only for:**
- Third-party webhooks (must be public endpoints)
- OAuth callbacks (like `/api/auth/[...all]`)
- Public APIs that need to be consumed by external services
- Streaming responses (Server Actions don't support streaming)

## Server Action Structure

### Basic Server Action Pattern

```typescript
"use server"

import { revalidatePath } from "next/cache"
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma"
import { z } from "zod"

// Define validation schema
const createItemSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1),
  email: z.string().email(),
})

// Define return type
export type ActionResult<T> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string }

// Server Action function
export async function createItem(
  formData: FormData,
): Promise<ActionResult<Item>> {
  try {
    // Validate input
    const validatedFields = createItemSchema.safeParse({
      name: formData.get("name"),
      email: formData.get("email"),
    })

    if (!validatedFields.success) {
      return {
        success: false,
        error: "Validation failed",
      }
    }

    // Perform database operation
    const item = await prisma.item.create({
      data: validatedFields.data,
    })

    // Revalidate cache
    revalidatePath("/items")

    return { success: true, data: item }
  } catch (error) {
    return {
      success: false,
      error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : "Unknown error",
    }
  }
}
```

### Server Action with Authentication

```typescript
"use server"

import { auth } from "@/lib/auth"
import { headers } from "next/headers"

async function getCurrentUser() {
  const session = await auth.api.getSession({
    headers: await headers(),
  })

  if (!session?.user) {
    return null
  }

  return session.user
}

export async function createProtectedItem(
  formData: FormData,
): Promise<ActionResult<Item>> {
  const user = await getCurrentUser()
  if (!user) {
    return { success: false, error: "Non authentifié" }
  }

  // Continue with authenticated operation
  // ...
}
```

## Using Server Actions in Components

### In Client Components with Forms

```typescript
"use client"

import { useActionState } from "react"
import { createItem } from "@/actions/item"
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { Input } from "@/components/ui/input"
import { Label } from "@/components/ui/label"

export function CreateItemForm() {
  const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(createItem, null)

  return (
    <form action={formAction} className="space-y-4">
      <div className="space-y-2">
        <Label htmlFor="name">Name *</Label>
        <Input
          id="name"
          name="name"
          type="text"
          required
          aria-label="Item name"
          aria-required="true"
          disabled={isPending}
        />
      </div>

      {state?.error && (
        <div role="alert" className="text-sm text-red-600">
          {state.error}
        </div>
      )}

      <Button
        type="submit"
        disabled={isPending}
        aria-busy={isPending}
        aria-label={isPending ? "Creating item..." : "Create item"}
      >
        {isPending ? "Creating..." : "Create"}
      </Button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

### In Server Components (Direct Call)

```typescript
import { getItems } from "@/actions/item"

export default async function ItemsPage() {
  const result = await getItems()

  if (!result.success) {
    return <div>Error: {result.error}</div>
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {result.data.map((item) => (
        <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

### With useTransition (Alternative to useActionState)

```typescript
"use client"

import { useTransition } from "react"
import { deleteItem } from "@/actions/item"
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"

export function DeleteItemButton({ itemId }: { itemId: string }) {
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition()

  const handleDelete = () => {
    startTransition(async () => {
      const result = await deleteItem(itemId)
      if (result.success) {
        // Handle success
      }
    })
  }

  return (
    <Button
      onClick={handleDelete}
      disabled={isPending}
      aria-busy={isPending}
      aria-label={isPending ? "Deleting item..." : "Delete item"}
    >
      {isPending ? "Deleting..." : "Delete"}
    </Button>
  )
}
```

## Migration from API Routes to Server Actions

### ❌ Bad: Using API Route

```typescript
// app/api/items/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server"
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma"

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json()
    const item = await prisma.item.create({
      data: body,
    })
    return NextResponse.json({ success: true, data: item })
  } catch (error) {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { success: false, error: "Failed to create" },
      { status: 500 }
    )
  }
}

// Client component
"use client"
async function handleSubmit() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/items", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ name: "Item" }),
  })
  const data = await response.json()
}
```

### ✅ Good: Using Server Action

```typescript
// actions/item.ts
"use server"

import { revalidatePath } from "next/cache"
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma"

export async function createItem(formData: FormData) {
  try {
    const name = formData.get("name") as string
    const item = await prisma.item.create({
      data: { name },
    })
    revalidatePath("/items")
    return { success: true, data: item }
  } catch (error) {
    return { success: false, error: "Failed to create" }
  }
}

// Client component
"use client"
import { createItem } from "@/actions/item"

export function CreateForm() {
  return (
    <form action={createItem}>
      <input name="name" />
      <button type="submit">Create</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Always Use "use server" Directive

```typescript
"use server" // Must be at the top of the file or function

export async function myAction() {
  // ...
}
```

### 2. Validate Input with Zod

```typescript
import { z } from "zod"

const schema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1),
  email: z.string().email(),
})

export async function createItem(formData: FormData) {
  const result = schema.safeParse({
    name: formData.get("name"),
    email: formData.get("email"),
  })

  if (!result.success) {
    return { success: false, error: "Invalid input" }
  }
  // ...
}
```

### 3. Revalidate Cache After Mutations

```typescript
import { revalidatePath, revalidateTag } from "next/cache"

export async function updateItem(id: string, formData: FormData) {
  // ... update operation
  revalidatePath("/items") // Revalidate specific path
  revalidatePath("/items/[id]", "page") // Revalidate dynamic route
  revalidateTag("items") // Revalidate by tag
}
```

### 4. Handle Errors Gracefully

```typescript
export async function createItem(formData: FormData): Promise<ActionResult<Item>> {
  try {
    // ... operation
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Error creating item:", error)
    return {
      success: false,
      error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : "Unknown error",
    }
  }
}
```

### 5. Type Safety with ActionResult Pattern

```typescript
export type ActionResult<T> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string }

export async function getItem(id: string): Promise<ActionResult<Item>> {
  // ...
}
```

### 6. Authentication Check Pattern

```typescript
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth"
import { headers } from "next/headers"

async function getCurrentUser() {
  const session = await auth.api.getSession({
    headers: await headers(),
  })
  return session?.user ?? null
}

export async function protectedAction(formData: FormData) {
  const user = await getCurrentUser()
  if (!user) {
    return { success: false, error: "Non authentifié" }
  }
  // ... continue
}
```

## File Organization

```
src/
  actions/
    item.ts          # Server Actions for items
    user.ts          # Server Actions for users
    releve.ts        # Server Actions for relevés
    session.ts       # Server Actions for sessions
    mesure.ts        # Server Actions for mesures
    export.ts        # Server Actions for exports
  app/
    api/
      auth/
        [...all]/
          route.ts   # Only for OAuth/webhooks (exception)
```

## Checklist

- ✓ All data mutations use Server Actions, not API Routes
- ✓ Server Actions are in `src/actions/` directory
- ✓ All Server Actions have `"use server"` directive
- ✓ Input validation with Zod schemas
- ✓ Proper error handling with ActionResult pattern
- ✓ Cache revalidation with `revalidatePath()` after mutations
- ✓ Authentication checks for protected actions
- ✓ Type safety with TypeScript types
- ✓ Forms use `action={serverAction}` or `useActionState`
- ✓ Loading states handled with `isPending` from `useActionState` or `useTransition`

## Exceptions: When API Routes Are Acceptable

- **OAuth/Auth callbacks**: `/api/auth/[...all]` (required by auth libraries)
- **Webhooks**: Public endpoints for third-party services (Stripe, GitHub, etc.)
- **Public APIs**: Endpoints consumed by external services
- **Streaming**: When you need streaming responses (Server Actions don't support this)

For all other cases, use Server Actions.
